WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.539 --> 00:00:04.970
Some of them might save Middle-Earth… and
some of them might destroy it.

00:00:04.970 --> 00:00:10.651
From fateful decisions to political intrigue
to the greedy pursuit of treasure, the characters

00:00:10.651 --> 00:00:13.799
of "The Rings of Power" have lived dramatic
lives.

00:00:13.799 --> 00:00:16.010
Here's what you need to know about all of
them.

00:00:16.010 --> 00:00:19.794
Galadriel is no stranger to fans of "The Lord
of the Rings."

00:00:19.794 --> 00:00:25.270
The Lady of Lórien is one of the most memorable
and important characters Tolkien ever created,

00:00:25.270 --> 00:00:29.960
and Cate Blanchett's portrayal of her in the
movie adaptations is nothing less than iconic.

00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:34.689
The version of Galadriel we meet in "The Rings
of Power," however, may not be the one you're expecting.

00:00:34.689 --> 00:00:40.020
This story begins in a time of relative peace,
after the defeat of the Dark Lord Morgoth

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:44.720
and the world-shattering War of Wrath that
closed out the First Age.

00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:49.440
Galadriel is considerably more brazen at this
point, as she hunts down the remnants of Morgoth's

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:51.850
forces to avenge her brother's death.

00:00:51.850 --> 00:00:56.350
After she completes her mission and abandons
her journey back into the west, Galadriel

00:00:56.350 --> 00:01:00.170
encounters Halbrand, a mortal man whose fate
is apparently intertwined with that of the

00:01:00.170 --> 00:01:01.470
future Elf-queen.

00:01:01.470 --> 00:01:04.659
Before that fateful encounter, though, she
spends time with Elrond in the Elven realm

00:01:04.659 --> 00:01:05.659
of Lindon.

00:01:05.659 --> 00:01:10.130
And eventually even sets foot on the island
realm of Númenor.

00:01:10.130 --> 00:01:14.290
Considering the breadth of these travels,
it's clear that this more reckless Galadriel

00:01:14.290 --> 00:01:19.028
is one of the main characters of "The Rings
of Power," if not the outright protagonist.

00:01:19.028 --> 00:01:24.760
During the Third Age, Elrond is one of the
most highly respected and experienced Elf-lords

00:01:24.760 --> 00:01:26.050
still in Middle-earth.

00:01:26.050 --> 00:01:29.460
You're probably well aware of his role in
Tolkien's more famous works.

00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:33.590
In "The Hobbit," he briefly allows Thorin
Oakenshield's company to stay in Rivendell.

00:01:33.590 --> 00:01:38.210
In "The Lord of the Rings," he heals Frodo
after the attack on Weathertop, hosts the

00:01:38.210 --> 00:01:43.350
Council of Elrond, and then takes one of the
last ships into the west.

00:01:43.350 --> 00:01:46.479
"The Rings of Power" features a very different
Elrond.

00:01:46.479 --> 00:01:50.520
At this point, still young and determined,
Elrond plays a part in the politics-heavy

00:01:50.520 --> 00:01:51.520
Lindon storyline.

00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:58.240
"It is said the wine of victory is sweetest
for those in whose bitter trials it has fermented."

00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:03.369
He also pays a visit to the Dwarves of Khazad-dûm,
suggesting the young Elf-lord's politicking

00:02:03.369 --> 00:02:06.330
may extend beyond his homeland's borders.

00:02:06.330 --> 00:02:10.029
You can expect far greater things from Elrond
as the series goes on.

00:02:10.029 --> 00:02:13.560
Throughout the Second Age, he proves himself
as a military commander during the Elves'

00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:18.739
conflict with Sauron, establishes Rivendell,
takes possession of Vilya, the Ring of Air,

00:02:18.739 --> 00:02:22.769
and helps overthrow the Dark Lord in the War
of the Last Alliance.

00:02:22.769 --> 00:02:26.439
There can be little doubt that these events
will be depicted in "The Rings of Power,"

00:02:26.439 --> 00:02:30.260
and that, by the end of the series, this young
Elf will have grown into the great leader

00:02:30.260 --> 00:02:31.980
he is destined to become.

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:36.340
One of the most exciting aspects of "The Rings
of Power" is that it will bring characters

00:02:36.340 --> 00:02:39.440
who have previously been little more than
legends to the screen.

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:44.300
Gil-galad is the High King of the Noldor and
ruler of Lindon, the Elven realm that lies

00:02:44.300 --> 00:02:45.999
in the western regions of Middle-earth.

00:02:45.999 --> 00:02:51.480
A wise leader and fierce warrior, Gil-galad
appears in a poem recited by Samwise Gamgee,

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:56.800
which tells of his keen lance, shining helm,
silver shield, who eventually falls into darkness.

00:02:56.800 --> 00:03:01.879
Indeed, you could reasonably argue that Gil-galad,
as a keen military commander and sworn enemy

00:03:01.879 --> 00:03:06.969
of Sauron, is the most important Elf to live
in Middle-earth during the Second Age.

00:03:06.969 --> 00:03:12.118
In "The Rings of Power," Gil-galad appears
to be exactly as magnificent as his reputation suggests.

00:03:12.118 --> 00:03:16.319
When Galadriel returns from her last mission
to the north, he presides over a ceremony

00:03:16.319 --> 00:03:19.489
granting her and her soldiers passage into
the West.

00:03:19.489 --> 00:03:25.209
He wears golden robes during the ceremony
and places a crown of golden laurels on Galadriel's head.

00:03:25.209 --> 00:03:29.884
Later in the season the Elf-king hosts a magnificent
feast for a few Dwarven visitors including

00:03:29.884 --> 00:03:31.229
Durin IV.

00:03:31.229 --> 00:03:36.589
These are mere hints towards Gil-galad's greater
importance, of course, but it seems certain

00:03:36.589 --> 00:03:39.981
we'll see much, much more of him in the coming
years.

00:03:39.981 --> 00:03:45.949
A skilled smith who witnesses the many calamities
of the First Age, Celebrimbor is best known

00:03:45.949 --> 00:03:47.819
for forging the Rings of Power.

00:03:47.819 --> 00:03:52.510
He first makes several lesser rings, crafted
under the tutelage of Sauron disguised as

00:03:52.510 --> 00:03:55.019
the Elf Annatar, the Lord of Gifts.

00:03:55.019 --> 00:03:59.379
Then, by himself, he creates the more powerful
Three Rings.

00:03:59.379 --> 00:04:05.560
After Sauron reveals himself and forges the
One Ring, things go south pretty quickly for

00:04:05.560 --> 00:04:06.560
Celebrimbor.

00:04:06.560 --> 00:04:11.109
In the second episode of the first season,
Celebrimbor personally asks for Elrond's assistance

00:04:11.109 --> 00:04:16.790
in building a tower that will house the most
powerful forge ever created by Elves, Humans,

00:04:16.790 --> 00:04:17.900
or Dwarves.

00:04:17.900 --> 00:04:22.290
It's a foreboding look at the master craftsman's
dark fate.

00:04:22.290 --> 00:04:26.850
Though we likely won't see his fall from grace
until much later in the series.

00:04:26.850 --> 00:04:30.730
Some fans have complained about Charles Edwards'
look as Celebrimbor.

00:04:30.730 --> 00:04:34.901
But however cranky they currently feel, fans
might rest a little easier knowing he is a

00:04:34.901 --> 00:04:37.930
renowned actor of both stage and screen.

00:04:37.930 --> 00:04:41.880
He's starred in shows such as "The Crown"
and "Downton Abbey," as well as productions

00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:44.610
of "Richard II" and "Much Ado About Nothing."

00:04:44.610 --> 00:04:47.680
In short, Celebrimbor is in very good hands.

00:04:47.680 --> 00:04:52.460
In 2019, "Deadline" reported that "Game of
Thrones" star Joseph Mawle had been cast as

00:04:52.460 --> 00:04:54.000
the show's villain, Oren.

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:58.800
Since Sauron is the principal threat to Middle-earth
during the Second Age, this led many to assume

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:04.200
"Oren" was a production codename and that
Mawle was, in fact, playing the Dark Lord himself.

00:05:04.200 --> 00:05:05.400
They were half right.

00:05:05.400 --> 00:05:09.430
As it turns out, thanks to a leaked character
sheet, "Oren" is indeed a pseudonym for a

00:05:09.430 --> 00:05:11.380
character named Adar.

00:05:11.380 --> 00:05:17.190
The sheet describes the character as an "Elf,
tortured, corrupted, turned [into a] warlord."

00:05:17.190 --> 00:05:23.830
Subsequent leaks and rumors suggest Adar will
lead an Orc-band hunting an enigmatic artifact,

00:05:23.830 --> 00:05:26.660
and that he will face off against Sauron.

00:05:26.660 --> 00:05:31.990
Most intriguingly, Adar may be revealed to
be one of Galadriel's brothers, perhaps the

00:05:31.990 --> 00:05:35.480
same brother she's avenging as the series
begins.

00:05:35.480 --> 00:05:38.780
This would be an astonishing break from Tolkien's
canon.

00:05:38.780 --> 00:05:43.420
Imagining Galadriel's brother as a corrupted
warlord borders on the sacrilegious.

00:05:43.420 --> 00:05:48.070
At the very least, it will be fascinating
to see how McKay and Payne might attempt to

00:05:48.070 --> 00:05:50.025
pull off a twist like this.

00:05:50.025 --> 00:05:54.281
Prince Durin IV is set to be one of the most
important Dwarven characters in "The Rings

00:05:54.281 --> 00:05:55.281
of Power."

00:05:55.281 --> 00:05:56.720
But who is he?

00:05:56.720 --> 00:06:02.110
Durin IV is a Second Age ruler of Khazad-dûm
and a bearer of one of the rings of power.

00:06:02.110 --> 00:06:03.890
His name is important.

00:06:03.890 --> 00:06:08.710
Every once in a while, a prince or king of
the House of Durin is born who is almost identical

00:06:08.710 --> 00:06:14.600
in appearance and manner to Durin the Deathless,
eldest of the Dwarves, who first awoke many

00:06:14.600 --> 00:06:16.270
millennia ago.

00:06:16.270 --> 00:06:21.260
These Dwarves are named Durin and are believed
by their kin to be a genuine reincarnation

00:06:21.260 --> 00:06:22.410
of the original.

00:06:22.410 --> 00:06:27.180
In total, six dwarves have been given the
name Durin throughout Middle-earth's history.

00:06:27.180 --> 00:06:30.930
While it's currently unclear what specific
part Prince Durin will have to play in the

00:06:30.930 --> 00:06:34.330
series, it will have something to do with
Celebrimbor's forge.

00:06:34.330 --> 00:06:39.420
And you can safely bet he'll be playing it
from within the kingdom of Khazad-dûm, later

00:06:39.420 --> 00:06:41.360
known as Moria.

00:06:41.360 --> 00:06:45.300
Tolkien writes in "The Lord of the Rings"
that even as Sauron's forces spread across

00:06:45.300 --> 00:06:50.580
Middle-earth, Khazad-dûm remained unconquered,
due to the strength and bravery of its people.

00:06:50.580 --> 00:06:54.280
Prince Durin will likely lead them for much
of the show's run.

00:06:54.570 --> 00:06:58.700
The Dwarven storyline in "The Rings of Power"
poses a curious question.

00:06:58.700 --> 00:07:02.770
If Durin IV is the prince of Khazad-dûm,
who's the king?

00:07:02.770 --> 00:07:04.840
The answer: Durin III.

00:07:04.840 --> 00:07:10.770
Despite the fact that, in Tolkien's writings,
Durin III and Durin IV live thousands of years

00:07:10.770 --> 00:07:16.450
apart, they'll be brought together as father
and son in the new series by its condensed timeline.

00:07:16.450 --> 00:07:21.760
This will avoid huge time jumps and associated
issues with human characters' mortality.

00:07:21.760 --> 00:07:26.180
"You've always had sandstone in your granite
as far as the elves were concerned."

00:07:26.180 --> 00:07:30.710
In the original text, Durin III has a much
more explicit role in the events surrounding

00:07:30.710 --> 00:07:33.150
Sauron's rise than Durin IV.

00:07:33.150 --> 00:07:37.970
This is the king who is first gifted a ring
of power by Sauron and who later leads his

00:07:37.970 --> 00:07:42.540
Dwarves into battle against the Dark Lord
before retreating to Khazad-dûm and closing

00:07:42.540 --> 00:07:44.330
the Doors of Durin for good.

00:07:44.330 --> 00:07:49.320
It should be expected that the relationship
between father and son will be severely tested

00:07:49.320 --> 00:07:52.760
as the days grow darker for the Dwarves of
Khazad-dûm.

00:07:52.760 --> 00:07:56.750
Rounding out the show's Dwarven protagonists
is Princess Disa.

00:07:56.750 --> 00:07:59.120
Currently, there's not a lot known about Disa.

00:07:59.120 --> 00:08:04.010
In the second episode of "The Rings of Power,"
she's introduced as Durin IV's strong-willed

00:08:04.010 --> 00:08:08.280
and loving wife, and the mother to two very
energetic children.

00:08:08.280 --> 00:08:13.580
A leak that occurred before the show premiered
suggests that Season 1 will include a Dwarven

00:08:13.580 --> 00:08:14.930
"religious sect."

00:08:14.930 --> 00:08:20.300
Given that Disa can be seen singing at a Dwarven
ceremony in one of the trailers, could Disa

00:08:20.300 --> 00:08:23.710
be a leader of this group or some kind of
priestess?

00:08:23.710 --> 00:08:27.300
How might she play into the politics of the
subterranean kingdom?

00:08:27.300 --> 00:08:30.710
What impact will she have on the fates of
the two Durins?

00:08:30.710 --> 00:08:31.907
Only time will tell.

00:08:31.907 --> 00:08:34.550
Arondir was created for "The Rings of Power."

00:08:34.550 --> 00:08:38.800
He's one of the Silvan Elves, who populate
the Greenwood and Lothlórien.

00:08:38.800 --> 00:08:43.370
But Arondir's journey takes him far beyond
these forest realms.

00:08:43.370 --> 00:08:48.220
Arondir falls into a forbidden romance with
Bronwyn, a human woman, while stationed at

00:08:48.220 --> 00:08:50.290
a watchtower in the Southern Lands.

00:08:50.290 --> 00:08:54.699
The storyline seems inspired by other Human-Elf
romances in Tolkien's work.

00:08:54.699 --> 00:08:59.740
Aragorn and Arwen in "The Lord of the Rings"
and Beren and Lúthien in "The Silmarillion"

00:08:59.740 --> 00:09:01.490
are prominent examples.

00:09:01.490 --> 00:09:06.729
Though it has yet to be revealed what troubles
might underlie Arondir and Bronwyn's relationship.

00:09:06.729 --> 00:09:11.380
"Twice in known history has a pairing between
elves and humans even been attempted and on

00:09:11.380 --> 00:09:13.430
each occasion, it ended in tragedy."

00:09:13.430 --> 00:09:16.949
Somewhat less murky is the setting for this
drama.

00:09:16.949 --> 00:09:21.839
Bronwyn's village, Tirharad, was created for
the series, but the name, literally "near

00:09:21.839 --> 00:09:27.699
Harad," suggests it's close to Harad, a mysterious
region located south of Mordor.

00:09:27.699 --> 00:09:30.839
The view from Arondir's watchtower reveals
a lot about this land.

00:09:30.839 --> 00:09:34.820
The geography of the valley below bears a
striking resemblance to the river Anduin,

00:09:34.820 --> 00:09:40.433
the hills of Emyn Arnen, and the Ephel Dúath
mountain range that makes up Mordor's western border.

00:09:40.433 --> 00:09:45.610
This could mean much of Arondir's Season 1
story takes place in and around the forests

00:09:45.610 --> 00:09:50.463
later known as Ithilien the perfect home for
a lovesick wood-Elf.

00:09:50.930 --> 00:09:56.510
An inhabitant of Tirharad, Bronwyn is a healer
and a single mother to son Theo.

00:09:56.510 --> 00:10:00.699
By episode two, there is still little else
known about the woman who loves Arondir.

00:10:00.699 --> 00:10:05.890
However, she quickly proves her resourcefulness
and determination when she saves Theo from

00:10:05.890 --> 00:10:08.750
a goblin that has burrowed up from beneath
her house.

00:10:08.750 --> 00:10:12.600
This occurs shortly after she returns from
her hometown which has been destroyed under

00:10:12.600 --> 00:10:14.760
mysterious circumstances.

00:10:14.760 --> 00:10:19.439
Using the severed head of the goblin, she
convinces her fellow villagers to abandon

00:10:19.439 --> 00:10:24.110
their homes in the event that Tirharad suffers
the same fate.

00:10:24.110 --> 00:10:28.100
Early in the series, Theo discovers a broken
sword hidden in the floorboards of a barn.

00:10:28.100 --> 00:10:30.519
The weapon is currently a total mystery.

00:10:30.519 --> 00:10:36.000
For a while, speculation ran rampant that
it could be Gurthang, the sword of Túrin Turambar.

00:10:36.000 --> 00:10:41.800
However, rights restrictions prevent Amazon
from using material found solely in "The Silmarillion."

00:10:41.800 --> 00:10:46.180
Now that we can watch the full episodes and
have gotten a good look at the design of the

00:10:46.180 --> 00:10:52.290
sword's hilt and pommel, and Sauron's mark
on the blade, this proves that it's definitively

00:10:52.290 --> 00:10:54.350
a weapon of the Dark Lord.

00:10:54.350 --> 00:11:00.079
It's clear that Theo has some kind of connection
to the wider events of the Second Age, and

00:11:00.079 --> 00:11:05.540
that the Tirharad storyline will reach far
beyond the secret romance at its core.

00:11:05.540 --> 00:11:10.350
Another character created for "The Rings of
Power," Halbrand is a mortal man running from

00:11:10.350 --> 00:11:11.389
his past.

00:11:11.389 --> 00:11:16.951
By episode 2, we still know little about the
mysterious figure, though he clearly has some compassion.

00:11:16.951 --> 00:11:22.139
As Halbrand and Galadriel attempt to survive
on a makeshift raft, the Elf is dragged under

00:11:22.139 --> 00:11:24.930
the waves during a powerful storm.

00:11:24.930 --> 00:11:28.389
Halbrand dives in and rescues her from a watery
grave.

00:11:28.389 --> 00:11:33.069
This of course is after he abandoned several
other survivors from his ship when it was

00:11:33.069 --> 00:11:35.119
attacked by a giant sea monster.

00:11:35.119 --> 00:11:40.664
In one of the character posters for the show,
Halbrand is seen holding a sword with a horsehead pommel.

00:11:40.664 --> 00:11:45.089
While it might be easy to assume this has
something to do with the Rohirrim, it's worth

00:11:45.089 --> 00:11:51.207
remembering that the Horse-lords of Rohan
don't exist at this point in Middle-earth's history.

00:11:51.207 --> 00:11:55.480
Halbrand's sword likely indicates he is Númenórean.

00:11:55.480 --> 00:12:00.529
Tolkien writes in "Unfinished Tales" that
the Dúnedain loved horses and riding and

00:12:00.529 --> 00:12:06.373
became so attuned to their steeds, that they
could call them to their side by merely thinking it so.

00:12:06.373 --> 00:12:11.040
There have also been images of him wearing
a tunic marked with a solar symbol similar

00:12:11.040 --> 00:12:14.089
to one found on another character poster.

00:12:14.089 --> 00:12:19.170
Fans have speculated this icon may belong
to the Númenórean noble Elendil.

00:12:19.170 --> 00:12:22.540
That's as far as our knowledge of Halbrand
goes, currently.

00:12:22.540 --> 00:12:27.319
It'll probably be a while before we find out
who he really is, what he's running from,

00:12:27.319 --> 00:12:29.368
and what he might become.

00:12:29.368 --> 00:12:35.259
The Stranger is by far the most mysterious
character in "The Rings of Power."

00:12:35.259 --> 00:12:40.209
He arrives in Middle-earth via fireball, which
can be seen streaking across the sky.

00:12:40.209 --> 00:12:45.050
The relationship between the Stranger and
the Harfoot Nori who first encounters him

00:12:45.050 --> 00:12:48.309
seems to be a central storyline for the series.

00:12:48.309 --> 00:12:52.670
The Stranger could be a wizard, but he probably
isn't.

00:12:52.670 --> 00:12:56.259
Even if he is, he almost certainly won't be
the one you're thinking of.

00:12:56.259 --> 00:13:01.660
Gandalf, Radagast, and Saruman all come to
Middle-earth in the Third Age, long after

00:13:01.660 --> 00:13:04.029
the events of the show take place.

00:13:04.029 --> 00:13:09.009
The Stranger might be one of the two Blue
Wizards, but it seems odd to introduce one

00:13:09.009 --> 00:13:11.980
with such fanfare and not the other.

00:13:11.980 --> 00:13:18.000
All things considered, it's not particularly
plausible that the Stranger is one of the Istari.

00:13:18.000 --> 00:13:19.740
So who is he?

00:13:19.740 --> 00:13:24.629
Well, you might want to consider a character
you won't find elsewhere on this list, someone

00:13:24.629 --> 00:13:28.649
who is guaranteed to play a major role in
the series.

00:13:28.649 --> 00:13:33.660
One of the most famous aspects of Sauron's
story during the Second Age is that he comes

00:13:33.660 --> 00:13:39.660
to the Elves disguised in a form so fair,
that few suspect his true identity.

00:13:39.660 --> 00:13:44.649
What better way of delivering a truly shocking
twist than to make the audience believe they're

00:13:44.649 --> 00:13:50.689
watching a kindly wizard befriend a humble
Harfoot, when they are, in fact, witnessing

00:13:50.689 --> 00:13:53.149
the return of the new Dark Lord?

00:13:53.149 --> 00:13:57.519
Nobody expected to see Hobbits in "The Rings
of Power," mostly because they don't show

00:13:57.519 --> 00:13:59.999
up in Middle-Earth's history until the Third
Age.

00:13:59.999 --> 00:14:03.899
And yet, here they are, or at least, their
ancestors.

00:14:03.899 --> 00:14:08.149
The Harfoots, like the Hobbits, are short
and have hairy feet, but that's where the

00:14:08.149 --> 00:14:09.680
resemblance ends.

00:14:09.680 --> 00:14:13.949
The Harfoots are a nomadic group, still searching
to find their place in the world.

00:14:13.949 --> 00:14:20.209
The show's featured Harfoots, Nori Brandyfoot,
Poppy Proudfellow, Largo Brandyfoot, and Marigold

00:14:20.209 --> 00:14:25.209
Brandyfoot encounter the Stranger on their
travels, which changes their lives forever.

00:14:25.209 --> 00:14:30.310
The series' depiction of this tribe of halflings
is almost entirely lifted from "Concerning

00:14:30.310 --> 00:14:33.680
Hobbits," the first chapter of "The Lord of
the Rings."

00:14:33.680 --> 00:14:38.089
This prologue explains that, in the so-called
"Wandering Days," Hobbits inhabited the upper

00:14:38.089 --> 00:14:40.929
vales of Anduin to the east of the Misty Mountains.

00:14:40.929 --> 00:14:41.939
According to Tolkien,

00:14:41.939 --> 00:14:47.949
"The Harfoots had much to do with Dwarves
in ancient times, and long lived in the foothills

00:14:47.949 --> 00:14:49.379
of the mountains."

00:14:49.379 --> 00:14:54.239
Only when the fortress of Dol Guldur rose
in the early Third Age did the Hobbits migrate

00:14:54.239 --> 00:14:58.510
westwards, eventually settling in the lands
that became the Shire.

00:14:58.510 --> 00:15:02.519
Clearly, these aren't the simple, merry gardeners
we all know and love.

00:15:02.519 --> 00:15:07.569
But the Harfoots' tale should nevertheless
offer up a tantalizing opportunity to explore

00:15:07.569 --> 00:15:12.309
the secret history of one of Middle-earth's
most remarkable races.

00:15:12.309 --> 00:15:19.291
"I can't help but feel that there's wonders
in this world beyond our wandering."

00:15:19.291 --> 00:15:24.199
Because of the show's condensed timeline,
we can assume the movers and shakers of late

00:15:24.199 --> 00:15:29.449
Númenórean history will be present from
early on, including Isildur, son of Elendil

00:15:29.449 --> 00:15:31.300
and future king of Gondor.

00:15:31.300 --> 00:15:35.800
At this point in time, Isildur is heir to
the lordship of Andúnië, a seaport located

00:15:35.800 --> 00:15:37.459
on the island's west coast.

00:15:37.459 --> 00:15:43.360
Notably, he is not yet king, and at the beginning
of the story, does not expect to become one.

00:15:43.360 --> 00:15:48.689
As the days grow darker and Sauron's shadow
falls upon Númenor, however, Isildur will

00:15:48.689 --> 00:15:52.639
doubtlessly be drawn into the Dark Lord's
deadly schemes.

00:15:52.639 --> 00:15:56.769
If you're familiar with "The Lord of the Rings,"
you know this ends tragically.

00:15:56.769 --> 00:16:01.569
One report claims he'll accompany Galadriel,
Elendil, and the Númenórean queen Míriel

00:16:01.569 --> 00:16:03.699
on a maritime journey to Middle-earth.

00:16:03.699 --> 00:16:06.819
Where exactly are they going and why?

00:16:06.819 --> 00:16:08.759
Your guess is as good as ours.

00:16:08.891 --> 00:16:14.209
Better known to "Lord of the Rings" fans as
High King of the Dúnedain and founder of

00:16:14.209 --> 00:16:20.129
both Gondor and Arnor, Elendil's rise to glory,
and eventual death at the hands of Sauron

00:16:20.129 --> 00:16:24.019
is sure to be a key storyline in "The Rings
of Power."

00:16:24.019 --> 00:16:28.569
Early on, though, he's merely a Númenórean
lord, albeit a powerful one.

00:16:28.569 --> 00:16:32.699
It's rumored that Elendil will head up recruitment
efforts and lead the Númenórean fleet on

00:16:32.699 --> 00:16:34.509
its voyage to Middle-earth.

00:16:34.509 --> 00:16:38.610
Tolkien writes in the appendices to "The Lord
of the Rings" that the Númenóreans began

00:16:38.610 --> 00:16:43.199
to settle in Middle-earth around the year
S.A. 1200, a few hundred years before the

00:16:43.199 --> 00:16:45.610
forging of the Rings of Power.

00:16:45.610 --> 00:16:50.869
Since the series condenses the timeline to
a single generation, it makes sense that Season

00:16:50.869 --> 00:16:54.279
1 will see the island nation spread its influence
across Middle-earth.

00:16:54.279 --> 00:16:57.000
Elendil, it appears, will lead that effort.

00:16:57.000 --> 00:17:02.559
As the seasons go by, expect to see him come
increasingly into conflict with Pharazôn,

00:17:02.559 --> 00:17:07.000
king of Númenor, as the latter falls under
the influence of the Dark Lord.

00:17:07.000 --> 00:17:09.560
"The past is dead.

00:17:09.560 --> 00:17:14.961
We either move forward, or we die with it."

00:17:14.961 --> 00:17:18.760
An epic saga is only as great as its monsters.

00:17:18.760 --> 00:17:23.089
While Sauron will undoubtedly be doing much
of the heavy lifting in this department in

00:17:23.089 --> 00:17:28.110
"The Rings of Power," Númenórean king Pharazôn
is nothing to be trifled with.

00:17:28.110 --> 00:17:32.720
Pharazôn the Golden becomes king of Númenor
after forcibly marrying Míriel, daughter

00:17:32.720 --> 00:17:35.160
of Tar-Palantir, the previous king.

00:17:35.160 --> 00:17:40.840
After Sauron conquers much of Middle-earth
and declares himself King of Men, an infuriated

00:17:40.840 --> 00:17:46.430
Pharazôn invades the mainland, swiftly and
decisively defeating the Dark Lord.

00:17:46.430 --> 00:17:50.940
When the Númenóreans take Sauron back to
their homeland as a hostage, however, he quickly

00:17:50.940 --> 00:17:54.370
gets to work corrupting them for his own ends.

00:17:54.370 --> 00:17:59.120
Pharazôn's subsequent fall into darkness
has world-changing consequences.

00:17:59.120 --> 00:18:04.170
It appears that at the beginning of Season
1, Pharazôn is merely a subject of Tar-Palantir

00:18:04.170 --> 00:18:07.620
and an advisor to his future wife Míriel.

00:18:07.620 --> 00:18:14.070
The show's Pharazon is a brilliant, authoritative,
and self-important type, which suggests the

00:18:14.070 --> 00:18:19.740
character's wicked personality is already
fully formed, even if his tragic fate has

00:18:19.740 --> 00:18:20.850
yet to play out.

00:18:21.166 --> 00:18:26.570
Míriel is the daughter of Tar-Palantir, the
king of Númenor, and the unwilling wife-to-be

00:18:26.570 --> 00:18:28.110
of Pharazôn.

00:18:28.110 --> 00:18:32.180
Tolkien wrote little about Tar-Míriel, aside
from the disturbing nature of her marriage,

00:18:32.180 --> 00:18:37.120
but it appears that she'll be a significant
part of the Númenórean storyline in "The

00:18:37.120 --> 00:18:38.120
Rings of Power."

00:18:38.120 --> 00:18:42.800
It appears that Míriel will experience visions
of Númenor's eventual destruction.

00:18:42.800 --> 00:18:47.020
It's also reported that Míriel will be a
queen during the events of the show, though

00:18:47.020 --> 00:18:48.940
her father is still alive.

00:18:48.940 --> 00:18:51.860
It's not yet known why this might be.

00:18:51.860 --> 00:18:57.170
It's said that she'll be a savvy, intelligent,
and compassionate ruler, and will see action

00:18:57.170 --> 00:19:01.260
in at least one battle after joining the Númenórean
expedition to Middle-earth.

00:19:01.260 --> 00:19:05.920
It's clear that the show's version of Míriel
will have much more impact on the history

00:19:05.920 --> 00:19:08.480
of Middle-earth than her literary counterpart.

00:19:08.586 --> 00:19:12.850
Tar-Palantir is unusual among the late kings
of Númenor.

00:19:12.850 --> 00:19:18.800
Though his immediate predecessors mistrusted
the Elves and the god-like Valar, he takes

00:19:18.800 --> 00:19:25.130
a royal name in the Elvish language Quenya,
as well as the Quenyan title "Tar," as opposed

00:19:25.130 --> 00:19:28.100
to "Ar," taken from the Númenórean language.

00:19:28.100 --> 00:19:33.280
This puts him at odds with his own people,
who are provoked by Pharazôn into open rebellion.

00:19:33.280 --> 00:19:38.770
After Tar-Palantir dies, Pharazôn usurps
the throne and marries his daughter, Míriel.

00:19:38.770 --> 00:19:43.040
Tar-Palantir is definitely in the show, though
he likely won't be a major player.

00:19:43.040 --> 00:19:45.840
We currently have no idea who'll be playing
him.

00:19:45.840 --> 00:19:51.245
Most probably, Tar-Palantir will be an elderly
ruler with diminished authority as the series opens.

00:19:51.245 --> 00:19:55.600
His main purpose will be to open up a power
vacuum, allowing the villainous Pharazôn

00:19:55.600 --> 00:19:57.639
to ascend to the throne of Númenor.

00:19:57.902 --> 00:20:03.270
"The Rings of Power" will feature several
ancillary Númenórean characters, all of

00:20:03.270 --> 00:20:05.450
whom are original to the series.

00:20:05.450 --> 00:20:09.880
The first is Eärien, Isildur's sister, who
is played by Ema Horvath.

00:20:09.880 --> 00:20:14.820
Eärien is set against Isildur's decision
to join the Númenórean expedition to Middle-earth.

00:20:14.820 --> 00:20:19.630
At one point, she chases him through the city
streets as he leads his soldiers to war.

00:20:19.630 --> 00:20:21.230
Finally, there's Kemen.

00:20:21.230 --> 00:20:26.340
As Pharazôn's son, Kemen will spend Season
1 involved in a romance with Eärien.

00:20:26.340 --> 00:20:32.140
Kemen is played by Leon Wadham, but it's unclear
what his role in the larger story of "The

00:20:32.140 --> 00:20:33.400
Rings of Power" will be.

00:20:33.874 --> 00:20:40.080
As Galadriel narrates the early days of Arda,
she speaks briefly about the Years of Trees,

00:20:40.080 --> 00:20:43.680
and the great wars that dominated the First
Age.

00:20:43.680 --> 00:20:46.550
Shortly after, we meet her brother, Finrod
Felagund.

00:20:46.550 --> 00:20:51.630
As her narration continues, it's revealed
that he is one of the great Elven warriors

00:20:51.630 --> 00:20:55.600
determined to end Morgoth's reign of terror
in Middle-earth.

00:20:55.600 --> 00:20:59.280
Much of the introduction to "The Rings of
Power" is condensed, likely because a majority

00:20:59.280 --> 00:21:03.700
of the story takes place during the Second
Age, but the particular battle that Galadriel

00:21:03.700 --> 00:21:09.580
references might be the culmination of, and
ultimate end to, the First Age.

00:21:09.580 --> 00:21:11.100
There's a lot to digest here.

00:21:11.100 --> 00:21:15.660
In Tolkien's writing, Finrod is an Elven king
and brother of Galadriel.

00:21:15.660 --> 00:21:20.750
He plays a key role in the events of the First
Age and eventually gives his life to save

00:21:20.750 --> 00:21:25.490
Beren from torture at the hands of Sauron,
who at that point is merely a lieutenant of

00:21:25.490 --> 00:21:27.010
the Dark Lord Morgoth.

00:21:27.010 --> 00:21:32.280
The battle in question could be the epic Dagor
Bragollach or even a glimpse of Beren's quest

00:21:32.280 --> 00:21:33.550
for the Silmaril.

00:21:33.550 --> 00:21:37.910
Unfortunately, most of this material is found
in "The Silmarillion," which means Amazon

00:21:37.910 --> 00:21:40.490
Studios can't use it for the series.

00:21:40.490 --> 00:21:45.560
Luckily, the appendices of "The Lord of the
Rings" do mention a few key details about

00:21:45.560 --> 00:21:50.490
Finrod, including his familial relationship
with Galadriel and the manner of his death.

00:21:50.490 --> 00:21:54.620
The odds are that Finrod's appearance in "The
Rings of Power" will be relegated to the show's

00:21:54.620 --> 00:22:00.120
prologue, which, with some clever trickery
and editing, could feasibly depict Middle-earth's

00:22:00.120 --> 00:22:03.720
ancient history without committing copyright
infringement.

00:22:03.866 --> 00:22:11.040
In May 2022, Amazon invited a number of Tolkien
fan blogs, websites, and YouTubers to a special

00:22:11.040 --> 00:22:15.900
event in London, where they were introduced
to the showrunners and shown footage from

00:22:15.900 --> 00:22:16.900
"The Rings of Power."

00:22:16.900 --> 00:22:20.660
Information about the event has since started
popping up all over the internet.

00:22:20.660 --> 00:22:25.350
According to several sources, show creators
Patrick McKay and John D. Payne told the attendees

00:22:25.350 --> 00:22:30.270
they have plans for all the characters in
the Second Age, specifically naming Celeborn

00:22:30.270 --> 00:22:32.740
and Celebrían as examples.

00:22:32.740 --> 00:22:36.560
These names will be familiar to fans of "The
Lord of the Rings."

00:22:36.560 --> 00:22:41.670
Celeborn is Galadriel's husband, while Celebrían
is Elrond's ill-fated wife.

00:22:41.670 --> 00:22:45.810
Neither Celeborn nor Celebrían will be appearing
in Season 1.

00:22:45.810 --> 00:22:49.880
For a long time, their absence from the cast
list was pretty conspicuous.

00:22:49.880 --> 00:22:56.680
Now, though, we know that these two characters
will simply be making their debut at a later point.

00:22:56.680 --> 00:22:57.780
This makes total sense.

00:22:57.780 --> 00:23:01.550
Considering how many characters are due to
appear in the series, it stands to reason

00:23:01.550 --> 00:23:05.150
that a few may need to be kept off the board
for a while.

00:23:05.150 --> 00:23:11.530
And, if nothing else, it's greatly reassuring
to know that the storytellers behind "The

00:23:11.530 --> 00:23:14.810
Rings of Power" know exactly where their tale
is headed.

